title: Writing Journal 2 — Who Am I As a Writer?
authors: Writing Journal 2 SBA, built in lo-blocks
description: A first-year ENGL 1010 student, unsure they count as a writer at all, is invited by Alma to the Writers' Circle, which meets in person on the library's second floor. The student arrives mid-conversation, meets Lee and Richard, learns the group's habit of describing themselves by "writer orientation," watches Alma talk through her own Writer Board, then builds their own (what they write and how often), works out why they write each kind, and finally reads the Circle's deck of eight writer-type cards and chooses one — then has to defend the choice with a story from their own writing life.
~~~~

Introduction
--------------
wj1_sidebar <- wj1_intro

// wj1_intro is a new page; its text is Katie's, from notes-for-piotr.txt
// ("wj1_intro [NEW PAGE]"), and now lives in writing_journal1_activities_part1.olx.

Alma: Hey, before we get started, read the text to the right. When you're done, come back and hit the blue continue button.

--- wait @wj1_intro_done.value ---

The Invitation
--------------
wj1_sidebar <- wj1_welcome

// Writing Team: "We want them to click a 'next' button at the bottom of wj1_intro
// before the chat can be advanced." Implemented above as the
// `--- wait @wj1_intro_done.value ---` gate in the Introduction section, backed by
// <Done id="wj1_intro_done" label="Next" /> at the bottom of the wj1_intro page.

You: hey — you took 1010 last year right [face=smile]

wj1_sidebar <- wj1_blank

You: what did you even do for the "who am i as a writer" journal. i've been staring at it all week

Alma: ha. i stared at it for three days [face=cheeky]

Alma: come to Writers' Circle on Thursday — 4pm, library, second floor [face=smile]

Alma: it's just students who get together every so often to talk about writing. what they're working on, what's been tripping them up. no pressure, come see [face=calm]

Alma: let me add you to the group chat

// Writing Team, verbatim: "You've been added to the Writing Circle Group Chat"
// OPEN QUESTION (Fable/Piotr to decide): how should this render? Candidates are a
// system/narrator message in the chat stream vs. a section header marking the move
// from the 1:1 thread into the group chat. Left unimplemented deliberately — no
// rendering invented here.

Thursday, 4:02
--------------

Lee: —and I didn't figure out what the paper was even about until I was two pages in. which is every time. that's just how it goes. [face=cheeky]

Richard: you say that like it's news. that's been every paper since last fall. [face=serious]

Lee: it's a process. [face=cheeky]

Richard: it's the absence of one. [face=serious]

Alma: okay, be nice — I've added a new member. My friend is taking Ellington's 1010 this semester. [face=smile]

Richard: ah. so you got the "who am I as a writer" journal. [face=serious]

You: yeah. I've been kind of stuck on it. [face=calm]

Lee: everyone's stuck on that one. it's a strange question the first time somebody asks you. [face=concerned]

The Introductions
-----------------

Alma: actually, before anything, we should do the introductions properly. not names. the other thing. [face=explaining]

Richard: the writer personalities?. nobody takes them too seriously. they're a shorthand. [face=serious]

wj1_sidebar <- wj1_orientations

Alma: I've shared the list of personalities. Look right. ---> [face=smile]

Alma: I'm a community writer 🤝 — which means I write primarily to interact with and connect with other people. [face=smile]

Lee: explorer 🧭. I write to learn and find out what I think about things. [face=cheeky]

Richard: technician 🔧. I write to get things done; writing is a tool for me. [face=serious]

Lee: you don't have to pick one yet, btw there's a whole list of them. you choose one at the end. [face=cheeky]

You: I pick my own? [face=calm]

Alma: yup. we used to guess for people. Richard would read your board and sort you. [face=explaining]

Richard: and then you'd tell me I was wrong, which was the useful part. [face=serious]

Richard: this is the honest version of that. you choose, and then you explain why. It is harder, but it's yours. [face=serious]

Richard: go ahead and read it. there's a **Mark as read** at the bottom. Click it and the chat will reopen. [face=serious]

--- wait @wj1_orientations_done.value ---

The Board Comes Up
------------------
wj1_sidebar <- wj1_blank

Lee: oh — Richard, I was going to tell you. that assignment I turned in Tuesday? I was stuck on the opening. sat there an hour. [face=concerned]

Richard: and? [face=serious]

Lee: I pulled up my writer board for some reason, and I'd forgotten I had "long replies in group chats" on there. which is exactly what the opening needed to sound like — someone explaining something to a person they already know. I wrote it like that, took twenty minutes. [face=awe]

Richard: that is the first genuinely useful thing that board has done for anybody. [face=serious]

Lee: it's bailed me out plenty of times, actually. [face=cheeky]

Alma: wait — have you ever made a writer board? [face=explaining]

You: I don't think so. what is it? [face=calm]

Alma: oh, then this is good timing. here — it's faster if I just show you mine. [face=smile]

wj1_sidebar <- wj1_alma_board

Alma: there it is, on the right ---> [face=smile]

Alma: so it's just everything you write, laid out. mine's ... okay. texting, obviously every day, mostly outside school. the roommate group chat, which is ninety percent negotiating whose turn it is to buy toilet paper. [face=explaining]

Richard: gripping. [face=serious]

Alma: the point is, it's still writing. emails go on the school side — professors, work, a couple times a week. discussion posts weekly, and I resent every one of them. captions when I post something. comments on shared docs when we're doing group projects, which honestly adds up to a lot of... [face=explaining]

Alma: huh. I always forget how much ends up on here. [face=awe]

Lee: everyone forgets. [face=cheeky]

Alma: no, but the first time I did this I put down three things. essays, emails, texting. I thought that was the list. [face=concerned]

Richard: and now? [face=serious]

Alma: now it doesn't fit on one screen. [face=smile]

Lee: don't worry, there's no quiz on Alma's toilet paper negotiations. hit **mark as read** when you're done [face=cheeky]

--- wait @wj1_alma_board_done.value ---

// PLATFORM/CONTENT REQUEST (later, not this merge) — Writing Team: "Each time we
// display the 'mark as read' button, can we have text appear once they click it
// that says 'go back to chat'?" Applies to every <Done> on the right-hand pages.

Your Turn — Build Your Board
----------------------------
wj1_sidebar <- wj1_board

Alma: okay, your turn. it doesn't have to be complete or organized — just put down what you actually do. there's a side for school and a side for everything else. [face=smile]

Lee: and count the stuff you don't think counts. that's the whole trick. [face=cheeky]

Alma: no rush at all. **Mark as complete** is down at the bottom for whenever you're ready [face=calm]

--- wait @wj1_board_done.value ---

There It Is
-----------

Lee: there it is. [face=cheeky]

// Set-before-announce: Alma's next line is the announce ("there's a spot on the
// right"), so the pane has to be showing wj1_board_noticing by the time she says it.
wj1_sidebar <- wj1_board_noticing

Alma: okay, real question — did anything end up on there that you wouldn't have called writing before you sat down? there's a spot on the right. [face=explaining]

Richard: that's the part that gets everybody. I had grocery lists on mine for about a year before I'd admit they counted. [face=serious]

Lee: they don't count. [face=cheeky]

Richard: they absolutely count. [face=serious]

wj1_board_ready <- go

Alma: oh — and your whole board just saved itself up top under **My Journal**. you can peek at the whole thing in one spot whenever. [face=smile]

Does That Even Count?
---------------------
wj1_sidebar <- wj1_blank

Richard: for the record — the grocery list thing. that's still writing. [face=serious]

Lee: Richard. it's a list of nouns. [face=cheeky]

Richard: it's a list of nouns, in a certain order, that does a job. [face=serious]

Lee: by that standard my phone's autocomplete is a writer. [face=cheeky]

Richard: not the item. the reason. I write a list to help me organize my thinking and accomplish a task. if you wrote the exact same list because it makes your head quieter, that's not the same activity. it just looks the same from outside. [face=thinking]

Lee: huh. [face=awe]

Richard: don't do that. [face=serious]

Lee: no, that's why the board actually worked for me on Tuesday. I didn't use "long replies in group chats." I used the fact that I write those to explain something to someone I already know. that was the part I needed. the entry on its own would've told me nothing. [face=thinking]

Alma: which is the thing about a board. any board. it's a list of what. it can't tell you whether any of it counts, or what a single piece of it is for. [face=explaining]

// Syntax: [face=...] must be the last thing on a dialogue line, so the face tag
// moved to the end. Wording unchanged.
Alma: so — go back through yours and put the why on it. Once you've answered the questions on the right, we can get back to the chat [face=smile]

wj1_sidebar <- wj1_why

// Lee's beat, restored from 66566b3: it enumerates the parts of the Why screen, so
// the student knows how long it runs before they start it.
// OPEN for Fable/Piotr: Alma's line above already ends "we can get back to the
// chat," which overlaps Lee's "we'll still be here." One of the two reassurances
// could go; the enumeration is the half that carries information.
Lee: fair warning, it's a longer one — the reasons, then the ones that come up most, then a few sentences at the end. we'll still be here. [face=cheeky]

--- wait @wj1_why_reflect.value && @wj1_why_top2.value.length >= 1 && @wj1_why_top2.value.length <= 4 ---

Enough to Run It
----------------
wj1_sidebar <- wj1_blank

Alma: okay. so now you've thought a bit more about what and why you write. Maybe lets look back at the writer personalities [face=smile]

Lee: took me about nine seconds. explorer. [face=cheeky]

Richard: nine seconds is not reading. you chose explorer because it gives you an excuse to avoid outlining. [face=serious]

Lee: and yet, its correct. [face=cheeky]

Lee: meanwhile Richard chose technician, which stunned this room to its core. [face=cheeky]

Richard: I took it because it's accurate. I read them all first. twice. [face=serious]

Lee: he did read them all twice. I watched him do it. [face=cheeky]

Richard: which is the actual advice. read all of them. people stop at the first personality that flatters them and call that a decision. [face=serious]

Alma: mine took me a while — pragmatist was close, honestly. what settled it was remembering one specific night I rewrote a message to my sister four times before I sent it. [face=calm]

You: what if none of them are me? [face=calm]

Alma: then you write one that describes you and name it. [face=smile]

Lee: students like you wrote every personality in that list, you know. they didn't come down off a mountain. [face=cheeky]

Richard: the personalities belong to whoever uses it. that includes you now. [face=serious]

Your Writer Type
----------------
wj1_sidebar <- wj1_profile
wj1_types_ready <- go

Alma: Check these out on the right side. The arrows move you through the personalities. there it is, on the right ---> [face=smile]

// Beat order (from 66566b3): the reference beat first, then the action
// instruction sitting adjacent to the gate it describes.
Lee: oh, and the whole list is up under the **Writer Personalities** tab now if you want them side by side. [face=cheeky]

Richard: After you click through and read each personality (twice like me!) click the **This one's me** button on the personality that fits you the best, or make your own. We don't move on until one of them has it. And don't worry you can change it after. Nothing locks. [face=serious]

--- wait @wj1_type_choice.value ---

Richard: Ok, so you chose it, now defend your choice. tell us a story. [face=serious]

Richard: a real one. what you were writing, what happened, what it took. [face=serious]

Alma: The story Richard's talking about should explain why you chose that personality. If the personalist only fits you part way, use the story to show us that. It's ok if you need to tell us more than one story. The part that doesn't fit is usually the interesting part. [face=calm]

--- wait @wj1_profile_reflect.value ---

Wrapping Up
-----------
wj1_sidebar <- wj1_wrapup

Alma: here's the thing to take with you — none of this is a verdict. it's a snapshot of what you write and why. the writing you're about to do in this class will keep changing the picture. [face=calm]

Alma: Your whole journal is gathered under **Print &amp; Submit**, up top; the button on the right will take you there. Give it a read, click the confirm box when you're happy, and export the PDF so you can submit to your class. I'm really glad you came to the writer's circle. [face=smile]
